  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStrong diurnal rises are underway this afternoon amid ample\nsunshine and warm air advection. High pressure supplying dry\nweather has become elongated along the eastern seaboard, which\nis anticipated to provide one more day of strong subsidence over\nthe forecast area before succumbing to an encroaching frontal\nboundary late Wednesday night into Thursday. Until then, expect\nanother hot and muggy day on Wednesday, with heat indices\nstretching into the upper 90s for much of the lowlands and\n70s/80s along the higher terrain.\n\nWhile turning more active for the second half of the work week,\ndiscussed in greater detail in Key Message 2, the heat and\nhumidity festers into the weekend. Unseasonably warm\ntemperatures remain planted over the Central Appalachians while\na frontal boundary remains draped to the north. Once this front\nventures through the area, which is currently not slated to\noccur until sometime late in the weekend or early next week,\ndaily heat and humidity is expected to triumph.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn axis of showers and storms draped along a frontal boundary,\ncurrently positioned through the Central Plains into the Great\nLakes, will gradually encroach into the forecast area for the\nsecond half of the work week into the weekend as influential\nhigh pressure erodes. This opens the door for daily shower and\nstorm chances starting as early as Wednesday evening, then\nincreasing in earnest for Thursday onward as the boundary\nresides in close quarters to the forecast area. Zonal flow aloft\nwill fail to push this boundary along, resulting in a multi-day\nstretch of unsettled weather.\n\nConvective signals do not yet reflect much in the way of severe\nweather with this feature. There will be greater concern once \nagain for high water issues that arise within this weather \npattern. Heavy downpours and training storms will tack on an \nabundance of rainfall over already saturated soils that will \nlead to localized to areawide flooding, seen repeatedly so far \nthis summer and early fall. This will be heavily dependent on \nwhere the axis of heaviest rainfall sets up each day. Latest \nexcessive rainfall outlook has removed the marginal risk that \nwas included for Wednesday given a northward trend in model \nrainfall amounts and the amount of dry air still hovering over \nthe region that precipitation will have to initially overcome.\nProceeding into the end of the week when rainfall trends are\nexpected to ramp up, the marginal risk will be deeply rooted\nover the forecast area, with upgrades to a slight certainly not\nout of the question.",
